body: "## What is already proven, and what is not\n\nThe SDK-first work has landed the hard structural half and it should be credited precisely, because the remaining gap is easy to mistake for something already closed:\n\n- `pm-9x6e` zeroed the import-boundary baseline. Verified 2026-07-26: `grep -rn 'from \"[^\"]*core/' src/cli` returns **0**. The CLI reaches nothing below the SDK.\n- `pm-e6tm5c` froze the exported surface behind a reviewed snapshot gate (`pnpm sdk:surface:check`).\n- `pm-cbwg` and `pm-w89s` proved, once, that a custom PM CLI can be built on the SDK alone.\n\nAll three answer the question *\"does the CLI reach below the SDK?\"* — a **downward** containment property. None answers *\"can the SDK do everything the CLI does?\"* — an **upward** completeness property. They are independent: a CLI can be perfectly contained and still hold behaviour no SDK consumer can reach, because the behaviour lives in `src/cli` and is never promoted.\n\n`src/cli` is 49,643 lines. That is where the answer to the upward question lives, and today nothing measures it.\n\n## The check that exists, and its actual coverage\n\n`tests/unit/sdk/action-schema-parity.spec.ts` is the closest thing to an upward check. Measured 2026-07-26:\n\n| | count |\n|---|---|\n| actions declared in `PM_TOOL_ACTIONS` | **85** |\n| cases in `SCHEMA_PARITY_CASES` | **10** |\n\nCoverage is **12%**, and it is a hand-written literal array:\n\n```ts\nconst SCHEMA_PARITY_CASES: Array<{ action: PmToolAction; command: string; flags: string[] }> = [\n  { action: \"close\", command: \"close\", flags: [...] },\n  ...\n];\n```\n\nConsequences that follow directly from the shape, not from any hypothetical:\n\n- A new command can ship with **no** SDK action and the suite stays green.\n- An existing action can lose a flag from its schema and stay green unless that exact flag is one of the enumerated literals.\n- The 75 unenumerated actions have never been asserted to match their command's flag contract at all.\n\n## This repository has already ruled on this shape\n\n`pm-w13j` — *\"ADR: literal all-src coverage supersedes curated include/exclude allowlist\"* — decided that a curated list is not an acceptable form for a completeness gate, because the curation itself becomes the thing that silently rots. That ADR was about coverage instrumentation. The reasoning is about the *form of the gate*, not about coverage specifically, and it applies here without modification: a 10-entry literal array purporting to gate an 85-action surface is the same failure with a different subject.\n\nThe repository also already knows the correct form. `pnpm sdk:surface:check` derives its assertion from the actual export surface. `pnpm contracts:check` derives from the contract table. `scripts/release/audit-package-boundary.mjs` derives from the real import graph. Three gates in this repository already do the derived thing. The parity check is the outlier.\n\n## What the gate must actually assert\n\n\"Reachable from the SDK\" needs a definition sharp enough to gate, otherwise this becomes a slogan. The proposed definition, in three parts:\n\n1. **Action completeness.** Every command action the CLI can dispatch has a corresponding SDK entry point. Derived by enumerating the command registry, not by listing cases.\n2. **Parameter completeness.** For each action, every flag in that command's `*_FLAG_CONTRACTS` maps to a parameter in the SDK action's schema, and no schema parameter is unreachable from any flag. This is bidirectional; the current check only ever looks one way for the flags it happens to list.\n3. **Behavioural completeness (the part with real teeth).** For a declared set of representative invocations, driving the action through the SDK produces the same result envelope as driving it through the CLI. This is what actually proves an SDK consumer can rebuild the CLI, and it is the part `pm-cbwg` demonstrated once by hand.\n\nPart 3 is expensive to make exhaustive and should not pretend to be. The honest form is a **ratchet**: a declared count of actions covered by behavioural equivalence that may only increase, starting from whatever the exemplar already covers. That is the same shape as the eslint-suppression and coverage-pragma ratchets this repository already runs, and it is the shape `pm-rtn5h6` and `pm-k6t4yb` both ask for.\n\n## Explicitly out of scope\n\nRendering is not behaviour. Human-readable TOON layout, help text wrapping, colour, and pager integration are presentation and belong to the CLI; the gate must not require an SDK consumer to reproduce them. The line is drawn at the **result envelope** — the same facts and the same structured fields — not at the rendered bytes. Drawing it anywhere else would make the gate unpassable and would be an argument for deleting it later.\n\n## Deliverable\n\n- A derived parity gate replacing the curated array, wired into the existing `static` gate matrix alongside `sdk:surface:check` and `contracts:check`.\n- The three-part definition above encoded as three separate assertions with separate failure messages, so a failure says which kind of incompleteness occurred.\n- A behavioural-equivalence ratchet with a committed baseline count and a shrinking-only-in-the-wrong-direction rule.\n- A negative-control fixture: a command registered without an SDK action must fail the gate, proving it can fail (`pm-k6t4yb`).\n- The 75 currently unasserted actions triaged into: covered by the derived gate, covered by the behavioural ratchet, or carrying an explicit recorded waiver with a reason. No action stays silently unexamined.\n\n## Relationship to neighbours\n\n- `pm-9x6e` (closed) proved downward containment; this proves upward completeness. Recorded as `discovered_from` because inspecting its result is what exposed the asymmetry.\n- `pm-e6tm5c` (closed) gates the SDK surface against unintended change. That gate protects what is exported; this one requires the right things to be exported in the first place.\n- `pm-w13j` (closed) is the governing precedent for rejecting the curated form. Recorded as `related` so the argument does not have to be re-made.\n- `pm-k6t4yb` requires every enforced gate to ship a negative control; this gate is one of its subjects.\n- `pm-m4ikkz` covers the MCP tool surface being a static snapshot blind to runtime schema. That is the same completeness question asked of a third consumer, and both should read the same derived registry rather than two hand-maintained lists.\n- `pm-xwah` derives MCP `inputSchema`s from `*_FLAG_CONTRACTS`. It builds part of the derivation machinery this gate consumes; sequencing them together avoids two traversals of the same contract table."
